    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-gqokli-hill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gqokli-hill.jpg" alt="Battle of Gqokli Hill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-gqokli-hill">Battle of Gqokli Hill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulundi_en_Gqokli-heuwel_(middel)_vanaf_eMakhosini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JMK, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the start of the nineteenth century the Zulu were a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nguni">Nguni-speaking</a> chiefdom on the White Mfolozi river, one of many in the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pongola-river">Phongolo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thukela-river">Thukela</a> rivers in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>. The region was dominated by two larger powers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mthethwa-paramountcy">Mthethwa Paramountcy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dingiswayo">Dingiswayo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ndwandwe">Ndwandwe Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zwide">Zwide</a>, competing for land, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> and control of trade with the Portuguese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shaka-zulu-king">Shaka</a>, a son of the Zulu chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#senzangakhona">Senzangakhona</a> born around 1787, grew up in exile and rose as a warrior in Dingiswayo’s army. When Senzangakhona died in 1816, Shaka seized the chieftaincy with Mthethwa support, displacing the designated heir. Around 1818 Dingiswayo was captured and killed by Zwide, leaving Shaka to take over the leadership of the former Mthethwa alliance. He resisted Ndwandwe attacks, traditionally at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-gqokli-hill">Battle of Gqokli Hill</a>, and around 1819 defeated the main Ndwandwe army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mhlatuze-river">Mhlatuze</a> river, after which Zwide’s people withdrew northwards. Shaka then absorbed the Mthethwa, Qwabe and many smaller chiefdoms, incorporating their young men into his regiments and their leaders into his court. Within a few years the Zulu chiefdom had become a kingdom ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kwabulawayo">KwaBulawayo</a>, the largest and most centralised state in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#durban"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durban.jpg" alt="Durban" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#durban">Durban</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Durban_from_the_Balcony_(Dennis_Sylvester_Hurd)_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dennis Sylvester Hurd, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Zulu society was organised in homesteads, <em>imizi</em>, each headed by a married man with his wives and children, who herded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> and cultivated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maize">maize</a> and pumpkins. Women did most of the farming, while men herded, hunted and served the king. Cattle were central to wealth and status: they were exchanged as <em>ilobolo</em> bridewealth, sacrificed to the ancestors and used by the king to reward followers. The royal herds, enlarged by raiding and confiscation, were kept at military homesteads across the kingdom and formed the economic base of royal power. Because regiments could marry only with the king’s permission, often in their late thirties, the king controlled the formation of new households and the labour of young men and women. Religious life centred on ancestral spirits, honoured by the king on behalf of the nation, and on the annual first-fruits ceremony, <em>umkhosi</em>, which renewed royal power and reviewed the regiments. Trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a> and, from 1824, with British traders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francis-farewell">Francis Farewell</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-francis-fynn">Henry Francis Fynn</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#durban">Port Natal</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bead">beads</a>, brass and cloth in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a> and cattle. Praise poetry, <em>izibongo</em>, preserved the deeds of kings and chiefs and remains a major oral literature in isiZulu.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-blood-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-blood-river.jpg" alt="Battle of Blood River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-blood-river">Battle of Blood River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloedrivier_monument.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Renier Maritz, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zulu army was built from age-regiments, <em>amabutho</em>, which gathered young men of the same age from across the kingdom regardless of their chiefdom, quartered them at royal homesteads, and bound them to the king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shaka-zulu-king">Shaka</a> is traditionally credited with introducing the short stabbing spear, the <em>iklwa</em>, the large cowhide shield and the encircling “horns of the bull” formation, in which a central body pinned the enemy while flanking horns surrounded it; historians now see some of these as developments of older practice. Under Shaka the army raided far beyond the kingdom, and his campaigns, together with drought and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>, are associated with the wider upheavals known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mfecane">Mfecane</a>, a term whose interpretation is debated. In 1838 Zulu forces fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-blood-river">Battle of Blood River</a> on 16 December a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#boers">Boer</a> laager under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a> repulsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dingane">Dingane</a>’s army with heavy losses. The army’s greatest victory came on 22 January 1879 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-isandlwana">Battle of Isandlwana</a>, where some 20,000 Zulu overran a British camp and killed more than 1,300 soldiers and auxiliaries. Defeats at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-rorkes-drift">Rorke’s Drift</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kambula">Kambula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-gingindlovu">Gingindlovu</a> followed, and on 4 July 1879 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederic-thesiger-2nd-baron-chelmsford">Lord Chelmsford</a>’s force broke the Zulu army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ulundi">Battle of Ulundi</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#dingane"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dingane.jpg" alt="Dingane" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#dingane">Dingane</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gardiner_-_Dingane_in_Ordinary_and_Dancing_Dresses_(1836),_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allen Francis Gardiner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Power was concentrated in the king, who controlled the army, the royal herds and the right of life and death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shaka-zulu-king">Shaka</a> placed his own relatives, especially royal women, in charge of military homesteads, and appointed loyal men as <em>izinduna</em>, officials who commanded regiments and administered districts. Conquered chiefs often kept their positions if they submitted, but their authority was overlaid by royal appointees and the regimental system. Shaka ruled harshly, and the massacres that followed the death of his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nandi-hinduism">Nandi</a> in 1827 alienated many followers. In September 1828 he was assassinated at his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dukuza">Dukuza</a> by his half-brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dingane">Dingane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mhlangana">Mhlangana</a>. Dingane (r. 1828–1840) ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mgungundlovu">uMgungundlovu</a> and in February 1838 had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#voortrekkers">Voortrekker</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#piet-retief">Piet Retief</a> and his party killed. His half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mpande">Mpande</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#boers">Boer</a> support, overthrew him in 1840 and ruled for more than three decades, a period of relative stability in which the kingdom accepted the loss of land south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thukela-river">Thukela</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#colony-of-natal">British Colony of Natal</a>. A succession war between Mpande’s sons ended in 1856 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ndondakusuka">Battle of Ndondakusuka</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> defeated his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mbuyazi">Mbuyazi</a>. Cetshwayo (r. 1872–1879) revived the regimental system and built his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulundi">Ulundi</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#mzilikazi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mzilikazi.jpg" alt="Mzilikazi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mzilikazi~detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Zulu Kingdom became one of the best-known African states in the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shaka-zulu-king">Shaka</a>’s career made him a figure of legend, portrayed as a military genius, a tyrant or a national founder depending on the writer, and the historian Carolyn Hamilton has shown how these images were shaped by both Zulu tradition and colonial writers. The kingdom’s rise reshaped southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a>: groups fleeing Zulu expansion or breaking away from it founded new states, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mzilikazi">Mzilikazi</a>, while the Gaza kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#soshangane">Soshangane</a> emerged from the defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ndwandwe">Ndwandwe</a>. Zulu military organisation was imitated by neighbouring kingdoms. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a> of 1879, and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-isandlwana">Isandlwana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-rorkes-drift">Rorke’s Drift</a>, became famous in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> and later in film, and it forced a reassessment of British confidence in colonial warfare. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-blood-river">Battle of Blood River</a> became a central symbol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalism, its anniversary later commemorated as the Day of the Vow and now observed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-africa">South Africa</a> as the Day of Reconciliation. Zulu identity, language and royal tradition remained powerful in twentieth-century South African politics, from the Zulu cultural organisation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inkatha-freedom-party">Inkatha</a> to the present monarchy. Today isiZulu is the most widely spoken home language in South Africa.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-isandlwana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-isandlwana.jpg" alt="Battle of Isandlwana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-isandlwana">Battle of Isandlwana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Isandhlwana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charles Edwin Fripp, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom’s end came from British imperial policy in the late 1870s. The high commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bartle-frere">Sir Henry Bartle Frere</a>, seeking a confederation of southern African states, regarded the Zulu army as a threat and in December 1878 issued an ultimatum demanding its disbandment. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> did not comply, British forces invaded in January 1879. Despite the Zulu victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-isandlwana">Isandlwana</a>, the army was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulundi">Ulundi</a> in July, and Cetshwayo was captured in August and sent into exile. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#garnet-wolseley">Sir Garnet Wolseley</a> divided Zululand among thirteen appointed chiefs, including the trader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-dunn-zululand">John Dunn</a> and the Zulu chiefs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zibhebhu-kamaphitha">Zibhebhu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hamu-kanzibe">Hamu</a>, a settlement designed to prevent the revival of royal power. Rivalries between royalists, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#usuthu">uSuthu</a>, and the appointed chiefs led to civil war. Cetshwayo, after visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#london">London</a> in 1882, was restored to part of his kingdom in 1883 but was defeated by Zibhebhu and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eshowe">Eshowe</a> in 1884. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dinuzulu-kacetshwayo">Dinuzulu</a> won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#boers">Boer</a> help against Zibhebhu at the price of land on which the Nieuwe Republiek was founded. In 1887 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> annexed the remainder as the colony of Zululand, which was incorporated into Natal in 1897. The Zulu monarchy survives today as a traditional institution within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-africa">South Africa</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Zulu Kingdom, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shaka-zulu-king">Shaka</a> from a small chiefdom after 1816, became the most powerful state of southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a> through its regimental army, and its defeat of a British force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-isandlwana">Isandlwana</a> in 1879 before its destruction at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ulundi">Ulundi</a> made it a lasting symbol of African resistance to colonial conquest.</em></p>

